    "thinking": "We are role C with goal \"OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity\". We have current source files knn.c and knn.h. The baseline is sequential (score 0.5, time ~2.09s). Our previous experiments: some valid ones like experiment 89c5107aac544c1f with score 0.64768 (~1.14s) using OpenMP parallel for static scheduling on flattened point array with per-thread local top-k arrays and linear merge. Another recent one (1540653195424c7a) had score 0.8662 (~0.323s). We need to propose a new experiment.\n\nWe have integration_source_ids empty, so sources_kept must be [].\n\nGoal: OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity. We should consider using reduction for the top-k? But top-k is not easily reducible with built-in reductions. However we can use custom reduction or per-thread arrays.\n\nWe have seen that flattening points across groups works well (score 0.64768). The best score so far from role C history is 0.8662 (~0.323s) which likely used a more efficient approach, maybe using OpenMP tasks? Or better scheduling? We need to propose something new.\n\nGiven the goal mentions \"reductions\", we could try using an array reduction for distances and labels? But that's not standard. Alternatively, use parallel for with schedule(dynamic) or guided to improve load balance if points are unevenly distributed across groups. The current best might have used static scheduling on flattened points. We can experiment with dynamic scheduling.\n\nAlso thread granularity: we could try using collapse(2) but earlier attempt failed due to not enough perfectly nested loops (the inner loop over j inside group i). But we can flatten manually and use a single parallel for.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate relevant to role C.\n\nHypothesis: Using OpenMP parallel for with dynamic scheduling on the flattened point array improves load balance when groups have varying sizes, while maintaining per-thread local top-k arrays and linear merge. This should reduce thread idle time compared to static scheduling.\n\nTransformations: Add OpenMP pragma, flatten loops, use dynamic schedule, allocate per-thread buffers once outside parallel region, merge after loop.\n\nWe must ensure correctness: tie-breaking by input order (group index then point index). With flattened array we need to preserve original ordering. Since each thread processes contiguous chunks in the flattened order, and we merge in thread ID order, that preserves global order because threads process disjoint contiguous segments of the original sequence. This is similar to previous successful approach.\n\nWe'll modify knn.c: flatten points into a single loop over all points across groups, but we need group label per point. We can create an array of labels and distances? Actually we can just iterate over groups and then points inside each group, but with dynamic scheduling on the outer i loop (groups) may cause load imbalance if groups have different sizes. Better to flatten.\n\nWe'll allocate arrays for all points: total_points = sum(g.length). Then fill a flat array of PointWithLabel structs? Or just compute distance on the fly while iterating flattened index. We can precompute distances? No, we need to evaluate each point against to_evaluate. So we can have a loop over i from 0 to n_groups-1 and j from 0 to g.length-1, but flatten into one index.\n\nWe'll use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) private(...). Each thread maintains local top-k arrays (distances_local, labels_local) of size k. After loop, merge using linear scan in thread order.\n\nNeed to allocate per-thread buffers: we can declare them inside the parallel region but outside the for loop so each thread allocates once. Use omp_get_max_threads() and allocate array of pointers? Simpler: use threadprivate or just allocate inside parallel with size k, then after loop merge into global arrays.\n\nWe'll need to initialize local distances to -1 and labels to -1 before processing points assigned to that thread.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Compute total_points = sum(g.length) (but we don't need it if we iterate over groups and points directly but flatten index). We can compute a cumulative offset array for each group? Or just use two nested loops with collapse(2)? But earlier attempt failed because not perfectly nested due to variable-length arrays? Actually the inner loop is inside Group g = groups[i]; that's fine. The error was \"not enough perfectly nested loops before 'Group'\" meaning we cannot have a declaration between the for and the next for when using collapse. So we can restructure: move group access into the inner loop condition.\n\nWe can do:\n\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) private(g, j, d)\nfor (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n    Group g = groups[i];\n    for (int j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\n        // compute distance and update local top-k\n    }\n}\n\nBut this is not perfectly nested because of the declaration. We can avoid collapse by just using a single loop over flattened index.\n\nBetter: flatten all points into one array with label per point. But that requires extra memory allocation. However we already have groups, so we can compute an offset for each group and then iterate over total_points.\n\nWe'll do:\n\nint *group_offsets = malloc((n_groups+1)*sizeof(int));\ngroup_offsets[0]=0;\nfor i=0..n_groups-1: group_offsets[i+1] = group_offsets[i] + groups[i].length;\n\nThen parallel for over idx from 0 to total_points-1. Inside, find which group and point index using binary search? That's overhead. Better to precompute an array of labels per point? But we can compute on the fly: use a while loop to decrement offset until we find correct group. Since groups are small maybe okay but not efficient.\n\nAlternative: keep two-level loops with dynamic scheduling on outer i, and inner j is sequential within each chunk assigned to thread. That's what previous successful experiment did (static). Dynamic may help if groups vary in size.\n\nWe'll try that: #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) private(g,j,d) firstprivate(distances_local, labels_local)? But we need per-thread arrays. We can allocate inside the parallel region before the loop:\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    float *dist_local = malloc(k*sizeof(float));\n    char *label_local = malloc(k*sizeof(char));\n    // initialize to -1\n    #pragma omp for schedule(dynamic)\n    for (int i=0; i<n_groups; i++) {\n        Group g = groups[i];\n        for (int j=0; j<g.length; j++) {\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, g.points[j]);\n            // update local top-k\n        }\n    }\n    // after loop, merge into global arrays using critical section or ordered?\n}\n\nBut merging with critical may be expensive. Better to have each thread store its own final top-k and then do a linear merge across threads in order of thread ID.\n\nWe can allocate an array per thread: float **dist_threads = malloc(num_threads * sizeof(float*)); etc. But we need num_threads known before parallel region? We can use omp_get_max_threads() or just allocate inside parallel and then after loop, each thread writes its local arrays to a global array indexed by thread ID using critical section.\n\nSimpler: Use reduction clause with user-defined reduction for top-k? That's complex. Instead we'll adopt the approach from previous successful experiment (89c5107aac544c1f) which used static scheduling and linear merge after loop. We can change schedule to dynamic.\n\nWe need to ensure that merging preserves order. With dynamic, chunks are assigned arbitrarily but each thread processes contiguous groups? Actually with schedule(dynamic), each iteration of the outer i loop is a chunk (by default chunk size 1). So threads get different groups in arbitrary order. But within a group, points are processed sequentially by one thread. However when merging per-thread top-k arrays, we need to preserve global input order: earlier points should be preferred if distances equal? The problem statement says tie-breaking is not specified but the sequential implementation uses insertion sort that preserves original order (since it inserts at first position where distance < existing or -1). So if two points have same distance, the one encountered later will not replace an earlier one because condition d < distances[x] strictly less. So equal distances are kept in order of appearance? Actually if d == distances[x], then d is not less, so it goes to next position; eventually if all positions filled with equal or smaller, it may be inserted at the end? But insertion only happens when d < distances[x]. If d equals some distance, it will skip that slot and go to next. So later points with same distance are placed after earlier ones (since they don't replace). That preserves input order for ties.\n\nWhen merging per-thread arrays, we need to combine them in the correct global order: threads processed disjoint sets of groups but not necessarily in increasing group index order because dynamic scheduling assigns iterations arbitrarily. However each thread's local top-k array is sorted by distance (ascending) and within same distance, points are in input order relative to that thread's assigned groups. But across threads, we need to merge all candidates while preserving global input order for equal distances. The standard approach: after parallel loop, collect per-thread arrays into a single list of size k*num_threads (or less), then sort by distance and use group index as tie-breaker? Actually the original sequential algorithm does not consider group index; it just processes points in order of groups and within each group. So global input order is defined by the sequence: all points from group0, then group1, etc. To preserve that for ties, we need to ensure that when merging, if two candidates have equal distance, the one from an earlier point (smaller global index) should come first.\n\nWe can assign each point