order for ties.\n\nWhen merging per-thread arrays, we need to combine them in the correct global order: threads processed disjoint sets of groups but not necessarily in increasing group index order because dynamic scheduling assigns iterations arbitrarily. However each thread's local top-k array is sorted by distance (ascending) and within same distance, points are in input order relative to that thread's assigned groups. But across threads, we need to merge all candidates while preserving global input order for equal distances. The standard approach: after parallel loop, collect per-thread arrays into a single list of size k*num_threads (or less), then sort by distance and use group index as tie-breaker? Actually the original sequential algorithm does not consider group index; it just processes points in order of groups and within each group. So global input order is defined by the sequence: all points from group0, then group1, etc. To preserve that for ties, we need to ensure that when merging, if two candidates have equal distance, the one from an earlier point (smaller global index) should come first.\n\nWe can assign each point a global index (sequential number). Then during local top-k insertion, also store the global index? But then merging becomes more complex. Simpler: use static scheduling with chunk size = 1 group per thread in order? That's what previous experiment did: schedule(static) on groups ensures contiguous chunks of groups assigned to threads in increasing order. So each thread processes a range of consecutive groups, preserving relative order within that range. Then merging by thread ID (increasing) preserves global order because earlier groups are processed by lower-numbered threads. That's why static scheduling works well.\n\nWith dynamic scheduling, the assignment is not ordered; we lose ordering guarantee. To preserve correctness for ties, we need to either use a different tie-breaking rule or ensure that after merging we sort with secondary key of original index. But adding global index increases memory and complexity.\n\nGiven our goal includes \"scheduling\", we could try schedule(guided) which also assigns chunks dynamically but in decreasing size; still not ordered. So static is better for ordering.\n\nAlternatively, we can use reduction on the top-k array using a custom reduction that merges two sorted arrays while preserving order? That's possible with OpenMP user-defined reductions. We can define a reduction operation that takes two per-thread top-k arrays and merges them into one (like merging k smallest from both). This would allow us to use #pragma omp parallel for reduction(...) without worrying about ordering because the reduction merge will combine in any order but we need to ensure tie-breaking. If we define the merge operation to preserve input order by using global index, then it's correct.\n\nBut implementing a user-defined reduction requires declaring a type and functions. That is more complex but possible.\n\nGiven time constraints, maybe stick with static scheduling which already gave good speedup (0.64768). We can try schedule(static) with different chunk sizes? Or use collapse(2) if we restructure loops to be perfectly nested. The earlier failure was due to declaration between for and inner loop. We can move the group access into a separate array or pointer.\n\nWe could do:\n\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) private(j,d)\nfor (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n    Group *g = &groups[i];\n    for (int j = 0; j < g->length; j++) {\n        // compute\n    }\n}\n\nBut still there is a declaration of pointer? Actually we can avoid declaring new variable: use groups[i]. directly. So:\n\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) private(j,d)\nfor (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < groups[i].length; j++) {\n        float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, groups[i].points[j]);\n        // update local top-k\n    }\n}\n\nThis is perfectly nested? The inner loop uses groups[i] which is fine. But we still need to declare private variables for the loops: i and j are automatically private if using #pragma omp parallel for with default(none) or shared. We'll use default(shared) but specify private(j,d). However d is declared inside, so it's automatic.\n\nBut we also need per-thread local top-k arrays. That requires allocation outside the loop but inside parallel region.\n\nWe can do:\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    float *dist_local = malloc(k*sizeof(float));\n    char *label_local = malloc(k*sizeof(char));\n    // initialize to -1\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static) private(j,d)\n    for (int i=0; i<n_groups; i++) {\n        for (int j=0; j<groups[i].length; j++) {\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, groups[i].points[j]);\n            // update local top-k\n        }\n    }\n    // after loop, merge into global arrays using critical section or ordered?\n}\n\nBut merging with critical may be expensive. Better to have each thread store its own final top-k and then do a linear merge across threads in order of thread ID.\n\nWe can allocate an array per thread: float **dist_threads = malloc(num_threads * sizeof(float*)); etc. But we need num_threads known before parallel region? We can use omp_get_max_threads() or just allocate inside parallel and then after loop, each thread writes its local arrays to a global array indexed by thread ID using critical section.\n\nSimpler: Use reduction clause with user-defined reduction for top-k? That's complex. Instead we'll adopt the approach from previous successful experiment (89c5107aac544c1f) which used static scheduling and linear merge after loop. We can change schedule to dynamic.\n\nWe need to ensure that merging preserves order. With dynamic, chunks are assigned arbitrarily but each thread processes contiguous groups? Actually with schedule(dynamic), each iteration of the outer i loop is a chunk (by default chunk size 1). So threads get different groups in arbitrary order. But within a group, points are processed sequentially by one thread. However when merging per-thread top-k arrays, we need to preserve global input order: earlier points should be preferred if distances equal? The problem statement says tie-breaking is not specified but the sequential implementation uses insertion sort that preserves original order (since it inserts at first position where distance < existing or -1). So if two points have same distance, the one encountered later will not replace an earlier one because condition d < distances[x] strictly less. So equal distances are kept in order of appearance? Actually if d == distances[x], then d is not less, so it goes to next position; eventually if all positions filled with equal or smaller, it may be inserted at the end? But insertion only happens when d < distances[x]. If d equals some distance, it will skip that slot and go to next. So later points with same distance are placed after earlier ones (since they don't replace). That preserves input order for ties.\n\nWhen merging per-thread arrays, we need to combine them in the correct global order: threads processed disjoint sets of groups but not necessarily in increasing group index order because dynamic scheduling assigns iterations arbitrarily. However each thread's local top-k array is sorted by distance (ascending) and within same distance, points are in input order relative to that thread's assigned groups. But across threads, we need to merge all candidates while preserving global input order for equal distances. The standard approach: after parallel loop, collect per-thread arrays into a single list of size k*num_threads (or less), then sort by distance and use group index as tie-breaker? Actually the original sequential algorithm does not consider group index; it just processes points in order of groups and within each group. So global input order is defined by the sequence: all points from group0, then group1, etc. To preserve that for ties, we need to ensure that when merging, if two candidates have equal distance, the one from an earlier point (smaller global index) should come first.\n\nWe can assign each point a global index (sequential number). Then during local top-k insertion, also store the global index? But then merging becomes more complex. Simpler: use static scheduling with chunk size = 1 group per thread in order? That's what previous experiment did: schedule(static) on groups ensures contiguous chunks of groups assigned to threads in increasing order. So each thread processes a range of consecutive groups, preserving relative order within that range. Then merging by thread ID (increasing) preserves global order because earlier groups are processed by lower-numbered threads. That's why static scheduling works well.\n\nWith dynamic scheduling, the assignment is not ordered; we lose ordering guarantee. To preserve correctness for ties, we need to either use a different tie-breaking rule or ensure that after merging we sort with secondary key of original index. But adding global index increases memory and complexity.\n\nGiven our goal includes \"scheduling\", we could try schedule(guided) which also assigns chunks dynamically but in decreasing size; still not ordered. So static is better for ordering.\n\nAlternatively, we can use reduction on the top-k array using a custom reduction that merges two sorted arrays while preserving order? That's possible with OpenMP user-defined reductions. We can define a reduction operation that takes two per-thread top-k arrays and merges them into one (like merging k smallest from both). This would allow us to use #pragma omp parallel for reduction(...) without worrying about ordering because the reduction merge will combine in any order but we need to ensure tie-breaking. If we define the merge operation to preserve input order by using global index, then it's correct.\n\nBut implementing a user-defined reduction requires declaring a type and functions. That is more complex but possible.\n\nGiven time constraints, maybe stick with static scheduling which already gave good speedup (0.64768). We can try schedule(static) with different chunk sizes? Or use collapse(2) if we restructure loops to be perfectly nested. The earlier failure was due to declaration between for and inner loop. We can move the group access into a separate array or pointer.\n\nWe could do:\n\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) private(j,d)\nfor (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n    Group *g = &groups[i];\n    for (int j = 0; j < g->length; j++) {\n        // compute\n    }\n}\n\nBut still there is a declaration of pointer? Actually we can avoid declaring new variable: use groups[i]. directly. So:\n\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) private(j,d)\nfor (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < groups[i].length; j++) {\n        float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, groups[i].points[j]);\n        // update local top-k\n    }\n}\n\nThis is perfectly nested? The inner loop uses groups[i] which is fine. But we still need to declare private variables for the loops: i and j are automatically private if using #pragma omp parallel for with default(none) or shared. We'll use default(shared) but specify private(j,d). However d is declared inside, so it's automatic.\n\nBut we also need per-thread local top-k arrays. That requires allocation outside the loop but inside parallel region.\n\nWe can do:\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n    float *dist_local = malloc(k*sizeof(float));\n    char *label_local = malloc(k*sizeof(char));\n    // initialize to -1\n    #pragma omp for schedule(static) private(j,d)\n    for (int i=0; i<n_groups; i++) {\n        for (int j=0; j<groups[i].length; j++) {\n            float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, groups[i].points[j]);\n            // update local top-k\n        }\n    }\n    // after loop, merge into global arrays using critical section or ordered?\